At 9:40 p.m. every phone in the building screams at once: stay inside, lock your doors, do not help the injured. You live on the 30th floor of a tower block. The lifts are about to die, the stairs are about to fill, and you have ten minutes to fill a bathtub.

We follow you day by day through 30 days of a zombie outbreak: a neighbour who will not leave your door, the day the power and water stop, a fire nineteen floors below you, a hallway count that only goes up, a knock from inside your own flat, and a message painted on a roof across the street that nobody finishes.

Along the way: how much water one person really needs, why the top floors of a tower go dry first, how long a freezer lasts without power, why smoke is the real killer in a tower fire, why you never light a flame in a sealed room, where a door actually breaks, and why you insulate from the ground before you insulate from the air.

Part 2 is coming.

